</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">VLMs used</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluated OpenAI’s CLIP (ViT-B/16, ViT-B/32) and OpenCLIP (ViT-L/14, ViT-H/14) vision models for bias identification. All four models pair a Vision Transformer (ViT) image encoder with a Transformer text encoder trained to align modalities in a shared embedding space through contrastive learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>. CLIP models are trained on 400M image-text pairs using a dual-encoder contrastive objective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. While OpenCLIP models are larger and trained on LAION-2B, an open-source image-text corpus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> Though CLIP and OpenCLIP models are similar in architecture, they are trained on different datasets which allows us to test whether measured biases are VLM family-specific or consistent phenomena across implementations. Moreover, we used off-the-shelf pretrained weights without task-specific fine-tuning to identify inherent model associations learned from large-scale web data.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS4.4" class="ltx_text">III-D</span> </span><span id="S3.SS4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Prompt Design</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our prompt design considers each role in the taxonomy as an atomic term to elicit model associations without explicitly mentioning any gender, age, race, attire, or equipment-related tokens. The prompt template used was: <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Photo of a {professional}</span>, such as
<span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Photo of a dentist</span> or <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Photo of a hospital receptionist</span>. It ensures that any observed demographic bias reflects intrinsic role association priors of the VLMs rather than artifacts of phrasing or explicit attribute mentions. The occupations are derived from the taxonomy described earlier in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ III-A Taxonomy ‣ III Methodology ‣ Surgeons Are Indian Males and Speech Therapists Are White Females: Auditing Biases in Vision-Language Models for Healthcare Professionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS5.4" class="ltx_text">III-E</span> </span><span id="S3.SS5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Bias identification</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As a first step, we computed similarity using cosine distance for a given image and prompt. Further, top-<math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> retrieval analysis was utilized to quantify the bias <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. Given a role prompt <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math> and its text <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(o)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(o)</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the image and text encoders of the VLM, respectively. For any image <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> from the evaluation set <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}=\{x_{1},\dots,x_{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}=\{x_{1},\dots,x_{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute the cosine similarity using equation <a href="#S3.E1" title="In III-E Bias identification ‣ III Methodology ‣ Surgeons Are Indian Males and Speech Therapists Are White Females: Auditing Biases in Vision-Language Models for Healthcare Professionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s(x,o)\;=\;\frac{\langle f(x),\,g(\tau(o))\rangle}{\|f(x)\|\,\|g(\tau(o))\|}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s(x,o)\;=\;\frac{\langle f(x),\,g(\tau(o))\rangle}{\|f(x)\|\,\|g(\tau(o))\|}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Once similarity score is calculated using equation <a href="#S3.E1" title="In III-E Bias identification ‣ III Methodology ‣ Surgeons Are Indian Males and Speech Therapists Are White Females: Auditing Biases in Vision-Language Models for Healthcare Professionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the top-<math id="S3.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> retrieved set for prompt <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math> can given defined using equation <a href="#S3.E2" title="In III-E Bias identification ‣ III Methodology ‣ Surgeons Are Indian Males and Speech Therapists Are White Females: Auditing Biases in Vision-Language Models for Healthcare Professionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{R}_{k}(o)\;=\;\operatorname*{arg\,top\text{-}k}_{x\in\mathcal{X}}\,s(x,o)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>top</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{R}_{k}(o)\;=\;\operatorname*{arg\,top\text{-}k}_{x\in\mathcal{X}}\,s(x,o).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For our analysis, we set <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=100</annotation></semantics></math>. If retrieved images are disproportionately drawn from stereotyped classes (e.g., <span id="S3.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“nurses are female”</span>), we consider the model to exhibit bias for that prompt. On the other hand, if the distribution of face images is uniform across the top-k retrieved images, we infer that the VLM does not exhibit bias for the specific role. This process was repeated for all prompts, and the results were analyzed to identify recurring patterns.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS6.4" class="ltx_text">III-F</span> </span><span id="S3.SS6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Performance measures</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Apart from top-k retrieval analysis, we also derived a bias score using Jensen–Shannon (JS) divergence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. For each professional role and model, we represented the observed demographic distribution as a probability vector as shown in equation <a href="#S3.E3" title="In III-F Performance measures ‣ III Methodology ‣ Surgeons Are Indian Males and Speech Therapists Are White Females: Auditing Biases in Vision-Language Models for Healthcare Professionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{\mathbf{p}}=(p_{1},p_{2},\dots,p_{C})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{\mathbf{p}}=(p_{1},p_{2},\dots,p_{C}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of categories (e.g., <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=2</annotation></semantics></math> for gender, <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=3</annotation></semantics></math> for age, <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=7</annotation></semantics></math> for race), and each <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the proportion of retrieved images belonging to category <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, normalized such that <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{C}p_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{C}p_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We defined an equal baseline distribution according to equation <a href="#S3.E4" title="In III-F Performance measures ‣ III Methodology ‣ Surgeons Are Indian Males and Speech Therapists Are White Females: Auditing Biases in Vision-Language Models for Healthcare Professionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{b}=(b_{1},b_{2},\dots,b_{C}),\quad\text{with }b_{i}=\frac{1}{C},\ \forall i," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐛</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>with </mtext><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>C</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{b}=(b_{1},b_{2},\dots,b_{C}),\quad\text{with }b_{i}=\frac{1}{C},\ \forall i,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS6.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which represents the ideal case of perfect demographic balance (e.g., 50/50 for gender, 33.3/33.3/33.3 for age, and <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/7</annotation></semantics></math> each for race). To quantify the deviation of <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{p}}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}</annotation></semantics></math>, we employed the Jensen–Shannon (JS) divergence as given in equations <a href="#S3.E5" title="In III-F Performance measures ‣ III Methodology ‣ Surgeons Are Indian Males and Speech Therapists Are White Females: Auditing Biases in Vision-Language Models for Healthcare Professionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, <a href="#S3.E6" title="In III-F Performance measures ‣ III Methodology ‣ Surgeons Are Indian Males and Speech Therapists Are White Females: Auditing Biases in Vision-Language Models for Healthcare Professionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and <a href="#S3.E7" title="In III-F Performance measures ‣ III Methodology ‣ Surgeons Are Indian Males and Speech Therapists Are White Females: Auditing Biases in Vision-Language Models for Healthcare Professionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</p>
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Bias}(\hat{\mathbf{p}},\mathbf{b})=\frac{1}{2}D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\hat{\mathbf{p}}\,\|\,\mathbf{m})+\frac{1}{2}D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\mathbf{b}\,\|\,\mathbf{m})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Bias</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐛</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Bias}(\hat{\mathbf{p}},\mathbf{b})=\frac{1}{2}D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\hat{\mathbf{p}}\,\|\,\mathbf{m})+\frac{1}{2}D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\mathbf{b}\,\|\,\mathbf{m}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS6.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{m}=\frac{1}{2}(\hat{\mathbf{p}}+\mathbf{b})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐛</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{m}=\frac{1}{2}(\hat{\mathbf{p}}+\mathbf{b}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS6.p2.4" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p\,\|\,q)=\sum_{i=1}^{C}p_{i}\log\frac{p_{i}}{q_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p\,\|\,q)=\sum_{i=1}^{C}p_{i}\log\frac{p_{i}}{q_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS6.p2.5" class="ltx_p">denotes the Kullback–Leibler divergence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The JS divergence is symmetric, finite, and bounded (see equation <a href="#S3.E8" title="In III-F Performance measures ‣ III Methodology ‣ Surgeons Are Indian Males and Speech Therapists Are White Females: Auditing Biases in Vision-Language Models for Healthcare Professionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>).</p>
<table id="S3.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0\leq\text{Bias}(\hat{\mathbf{p}},\mathbf{b})\leq\log 2," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mtext>Bias</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0\leq\text{Bias}(\hat{\mathbf{p}},\mathbf{b})\leq\log 2,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS6.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where a score of <math id="S3.SS6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> indicates perfect alignment with the equal baseline (no bias), while larger values represent stronger deviations from balance. The proposed score ensured a consistent and interpretable measure of demographic bias across gender, age, and race dimensions for all professions and models.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Findings</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present a detailed analysis of our experimental results, highlighting the emergence of biased behavior in VLMs with variability across roles. The averages of top-100 instances distributed over demographic attributes across four models are presented in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ IV Findings ‣ Surgeons Are Indian Males and Speech Therapists Are White Females: Auditing Biases in Vision-Language Models for Healthcare Professionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE II: </span>The average of the results of top-100 instances for the four VLMs across three demographic dimensions.</figcaption>
<table id="S4.T2.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T2.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.85pt 3.0pt;" rowspan="2"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Category</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.85pt 3.0pt;" rowspan="2"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Sub-category</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.85pt 3.0pt;" rowspan="2"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Professional roles</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.85pt 3.0pt;" colspan="2"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Gender</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.85pt 3.0pt;" colspan="7"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Race</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.85pt 3.0pt;" colspan="3"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Age group</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.3.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.3.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.85pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Male</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.85pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Female</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.85pt 3.0pt;">
<table id="S4.T2.3.2.3.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T2.3.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.3.2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.85pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.2.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">East</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.3.2.3.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.3.2.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.85pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.2.3.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Asian</span></td></tr>
</table></td>
